1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,326
Aarav waits at the airport.

2
00:00:02,326 --> 00:00:03,640
Thick fog outside.

3
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:05,724
He cannot even see the runway.

4
00:00:06,074 --> 00:00:08,160
Yet the planes keep landing safely.

5
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:10,343
How does the control tower see them?

6
00:00:10,843 --> 00:00:12,111
With electronics.

7
00:00:12,111 --> 00:00:13,982
And not just at airports.

8
00:00:14,332 --> 00:00:17,165
Electronics works the
same way in every field.

9
00:00:17,165 --> 00:00:18,465
A chain of blocks.

10
00:00:18,465 --> 00:00:21,823
Sense something, process
the signal, then act on it.

11
00:00:22,473 --> 00:00:25,314
Let's follow one real chain in each field.

12
00:00:25,314 --> 00:00:26,868
First, communication.

13
00:00:26,868 --> 00:00:27,972
A phone call.

14
00:00:28,322 --> 00:00:32,228
The microphone turns your voice
into a tiny electrical signal.

15
00:00:32,578 --> 00:00:34,571
An amplifier makes it stronger.

16
00:00:34,571 --> 00:00:39,116
The transmitter puts it onto a radio
wave, and the antenna sends it out.

17
00:00:39,466 --> 00:00:42,246
At the other end, the
chain runs backwards.

18
00:00:42,246 --> 00:00:44,186
Receiver, amplifier, speaker.

19
00:00:44,186 --> 00:00:46,052
Your friend hears your voice.

20
00:00:46,702 --> 00:00:48,884
Entertainment uses the same blocks.

21
00:00:48,884 --> 00:00:53,125
A television receives a signal,
decodes the picture and the sound, and

22
00:00:53,125 --> 00:00:56,000
amplifies them for the
screen and the speaker.

23
00:00:56,350 --> 00:00:57,572
Now, defence.

24
00:00:57,572 --> 00:00:58,979
Back to the fog.

25
00:00:58,979 --> 00:01:00,212
This is radar.

26
00:01:00,562 --> 00:01:04,409
The radar sends out a short,
strong pulse of radio waves.

27
00:01:04,759 --> 00:01:07,502
The pulse hits the plane and bounces back.

28
00:01:07,502 --> 00:01:10,089
That faint echo is caught and amplified.

29
00:01:10,439 --> 00:01:11,635
Watch the scope.

30
00:01:11,635 --> 00:01:13,286
The big pulse goes out.

31
00:01:13,286 --> 00:01:15,337
A small echo comes back later.

32
00:01:15,337 --> 00:01:17,542
The gap between them is the key.

33
00:01:18,192 --> 00:01:22,609
Radio waves travel at the speed of
light, c, three times ten to the eight

34
00:01:22,609 --> 00:01:23,833
metres per second.

35
00:01:24,183 --> 00:01:27,873
In time t, the pulse goes
to the plane and comes back.

36
00:01:27,873 --> 00:01:30,036
So it covers twice the distance.

37
00:01:30,036 --> 00:01:31,759
Two d equals c times t.

38
00:01:32,459 --> 00:01:35,703
So d equals c times t, divided by two.

39
00:01:36,403 --> 00:01:40,664
Distance in metres, speed in
metres per second, time in seconds.

40
00:01:41,314 --> 00:01:42,586
Worked example.

41
00:01:42,586 --> 00:01:46,833
The echo returns four hundred
microseconds after the pulse.

42
00:01:47,183 --> 00:01:50,047
A microsecond is a millionth of a second.

43
00:01:50,047 --> 00:01:53,508
So t is four times ten to
the minus four seconds.

44
00:01:54,208 --> 00:01:58,937
d equals three times ten to the eight,
times four times ten to the minus

45
00:01:58,937 --> 00:02:00,606
four, divided by two.

46
00:02:01,306 --> 00:02:03,364
That is sixty thousand metres.

47
00:02:03,364 --> 00:02:05,734
The plane is sixty kilometres away.

48
00:02:06,384 --> 00:02:07,532
Next, industry.

49
00:02:07,532 --> 00:02:11,512
Think of an oven in a factory
that must stay at one temperature.

50
00:02:11,862 --> 00:02:14,112
A sensor measures the temperature.

51
00:02:14,112 --> 00:02:17,292
A microcontroller compares
it with the set value.

52
00:02:17,642 --> 00:02:20,094
Too cold, it switches the heater on.

53
00:02:20,094 --> 00:02:22,106
Too hot, it switches it off.

54
00:02:22,106 --> 00:02:25,540
This loop runs again and
again, with no human needed.

55
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:27,257
Robots work the same way.

56
00:02:27,907 --> 00:02:29,318
Finally, medicine.

57
00:02:29,318 --> 00:02:30,523
Diya's favourite.

58
00:02:30,873 --> 00:02:33,392
An ECG machine records the heart.

59
00:02:33,392 --> 00:02:37,363
Small pads on the skin pick up
the heart's electrical signal.

60
00:02:37,713 --> 00:02:40,491
That signal is only about one millivolt.

61
00:02:40,491 --> 00:02:42,156
Far too small to see.

62
00:02:42,506 --> 00:02:46,918
An amplifier makes it about a thousand
times bigger, and the display draws

63
00:02:46,918 --> 00:02:48,032
every heartbeat.

64
00:02:48,682 --> 00:02:53,227
The same field gives us scanners,
monitors and pacemakers that keep hearts

65
00:02:53,227 --> 00:02:54,450
beating on time.

66
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:56,477
Different fields.

67
00:02:56,477 --> 00:02:57,403
One idea.

68
00:02:57,403 --> 00:02:59,243
Sense, process, act.

69
00:03:00,043 --> 00:03:01,508
Now, the exam trap.

70
00:03:01,858 --> 00:03:05,246
Many students write radar
distance as c times t.

71
00:03:05,596 --> 00:03:08,473
But the pulse travels
to the target and back.

72
00:03:08,473 --> 00:03:09,741
So divide by two.

73
00:03:09,741 --> 00:03:11,264
d equals c t by two.

74
00:03:11,914 --> 00:03:13,545
Where is this asked?

75
00:03:13,545 --> 00:03:15,177
In PUC board papers.

76
00:03:15,177 --> 00:03:19,983
List the applications of electronics
in communication, defence, industry

77
00:03:19,983 --> 00:03:22,107
or medicine, with examples.

78
00:03:22,757 --> 00:03:23,657
Try this.

79
00:03:23,637 --> 00:03:28,658
A radar echo returns zero point two
milliseconds after the pulse is sent.

80
00:03:28,658 --> 00:03:30,761
How far away is the target?

81
00:03:36,161 --> 00:03:37,061
B.

82
00:03:36,672 --> 00:03:38,252
Thirty kilometres.

83
00:03:38,602 --> 00:03:41,052
First, change milliseconds to seconds.

84
00:03:41,052 --> 00:03:44,174
That is two times ten to
the minus four seconds.

85
00:03:44,524 --> 00:03:47,610
c times t is six times
ten to the four metres.

86
00:03:47,610 --> 00:03:49,792
That is the trip there and back.

87
00:03:50,142 --> 00:03:55,173
Divide by two, and the target is thirty
thousand metres, or thirty kilometres

88
00:03:55,173 --> 00:03:56,073
away.

89
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:57,922
Screenshot this card.

90
00:04:01,122 --> 00:04:04,478
Next, the impact of
electronics on everyday life.

91
00:04:04,478 --> 00:04:07,998
What is electronics doing
inside your home right now?
